From 55afc374e8a589bf32062974acd2483a90a28109 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: linux-a9 <linux-a9@localhost>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 01:10:12 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] psci: Minimal EL3 PSCI for A7-A9, and a re-entry point from
 Linux

On A7-A9 m1n1 is entered at EL3 and stays resident: its image and the
per-CPU EL3 stacks are memreserved, and VBAR_EL3 points at its vectors
while Linux runs at EL1. Use that to give Linux PSCI 1.0 CPU_ON, CPU_OFF
and AFFINITY_INFO (plus VERSION, FEATURES and MIGRATE_INFO_TYPE). The
spin table has no cpu_die, so without this the kernel refuses kexec on
an SMP system, can't offline CPUs, and a crash kernel inherits CPUs stuck
in the old kernel.

CPU_OFF does not power the core down. It cleans and invalidates its L1D,
stops its timers, and waits in WFE at EL3 (MMU off) on a one-line
mailbox. CPU_ON posts an entry point; the core leaves with a clean
I-cache and TLB, SCTLR_EL1 at its reset value, DAIF masked and SP_EL3 on
a static per-CPU stack. SYSTEM_OFF/SYSTEM_RESET, CPU_SUSPEND and
SMCCC_VERSION return NOT_SUPPORTED, so callers fall back to their own
methods and assume SMCCC 1.0.

Opt-in with the payload variable psci=1, and only used when m1n1 itself
was entered at EL3. kboot then parks the secondaries in the pen (a core
that doesn't park is pruned), sets enable-method = "psci" on every CPU,
adds /psci (method = "smc") and records the re-entry address and the
original boot_args in /chosen.

Re-entry: _reentry can be jumped to from a kexec'd trampoline (EL1, MMU
off, x0 = boot_args copy). It drops this image's lines from the caches,
flags the re-entry and restarts m1n1, which then skips the (stale)
payload area and runs the proxy. smp_start_secondaries() takes cores
back from the pen with CPU_ON, and the mailboxes and the psci=1 setting
live in .data so they survive the .bss clear.
